---
source: crates/api/tests/edits.rs
assertion_line: 183
expression: request.instruction
---
Edit the first image. The second image is a rough, low-quality preview showing the desired new geometry (shape, size and position) of the edited object or objects; it contains artifacts such as stretched texture and filled gaps, so use it only for geometry and ignore those artifacts. Make the couch about 40% shorter, keeping its left end fixed. First work out what each edited object actually is (what kind of object, what it is made of, how it is built), then change it the way a real object of that kind would look at the new size and position: keep the proportions of its parts, the scale of its texture and pattern, its edges, reflections, highlights, shadows and perspective physically believable, so the result looks like an untouched photograph of a real object, not an edited or distorted picture. Do not stretch or squash textures, patterns or details; make an object longer or shorter by extending or removing material the way a real one would be built. Redraw whatever the old shape covered or the new shape uncovers so it looks natural. Keep the colours, saturation and brightness of the object exactly as in the original, with no fading, washing out or tint. Reproduce the geometry shown in the second image, including the direction of any turn: if the object leans or rotates there, it must lean or rotate the same way and by the same amount, and never the opposite way. The edited object must always be shown complete and natural: never cropped, cut off, clipped by a straight edge or squared off at the border of the edited area, and never overlapping or hiding other objects unnaturally. If it grows or moves, redraw its full outline, extend it into the surroundings and fill the space it now covers, with correct contact shadows. Everything outside the edited objects must stay identical to the original. Output only the full edited image with the same framing and aspect ratio as the original.
